proofs.proof_tex:
At \(h=0\), no interference and the definition of the local potential-outcome array give
\[
Y^{(0)}_{nksi}(1)=Y^{(0)}_{nksi}(0)=B_{nksi}
\qquad(k\in K,\ s\in S,\ i\le N_{nks}).
\]
Consequently the Fisher completion equals the fixed baseline value on every observed coordinate, independently of the realized actions.  The random-holdout assumption and stage--\(1\) complete randomization assign to a candidate first action \(((H'_{n,s},z'_{n1s}))_{s\in S}\) the mass
\[
\prod_{s\in S}
\left\{
\binom{N_{n1s}}{\lfloor\rho N_{n1s}\rfloor}
\binom{N_{n1s}}{m_{n1s}}
\right\}^{-1}.
\]
The thresholds are fixed, so the selected label is precisely the deterministic recomputation \(C'_n(\rho)\).  Conditional on that label being \(C\), the stage--\(2\) complete-randomization assumption supplies independent uniform quota-respecting assignments only for \(s\) satisfying \(C_s=1\).  By \(\mathrm{lem{:}explicit\mbox{-}candidate\mbox{-}support}\), their joint support is exactly \(\Omega_{n,C}(\mathcal D_n^{0,\rho},\rho)\).  Bayes' formula therefore yields, for each supported history \(\omega\),
\[
\mathsf P_n^{0,\rho}\{\omega\mid E_{n,C}(\rho)\}
=\frac{1}{p_{\mathrm{ref}}(n,C,\mathcal D_n^{0,\rho},\rho)}
\prod_{s\in S}
\left\{
\binom{N_{n1s}}{\lfloor\rho N_{n1s}\rfloor}
\binom{N_{n1s}}{m_{n1s}}
\right\}^{-1}
\prod_{s:C_s=1}\binom{N_{n2s}}{m_{n2s}}^{-1}.
\]
This is exactly \(\mathsf R_{n,\mathcal D(\rho)}^{0,C}\{\omega\}\).  Every division is valid by the displayed premise \(p_{\mathrm{ref}}>0\); finiteness and nonemptiness of \(S\) make all products finite and the cell meaningful.

It remains only to verify the finite-support tail calculation.  On any finite probability space with statistic \(T\), let \(T'\) be an independent draw from the same law and define \(p(T)=P\{T'\ge T\}\).  If the distinct values are \(t_1>\cdots>t_L\) with masses \(w_l\), then \(p(t_l)=\sum_{a\le l}w_a\), and hence, for \(0\le u\le1\),
\[
P\{p(T)\le u\}
=\sum_{l:\,\sum_{a\le l}w_a\le u}w_l\le u.
\]
Thus ties are conservative.  Applying this inequality under the conditional law identified above proves superuniformity conditional on \(E_{n,C}(\rho)\).  Equivalently, for the generalized upper critical value \(q\),
\[
\mathsf P_n^{0,\rho}\{T_j(\theta_j)>q\mid E_{n,C}(\rho)\}\le\alpha.
\]
This is the asserted exact finite-sample selective level.

proposed.construction:
For fixed \(\rho\in I\), define the first augmented randomized action by
\[
\mathcal A_{n,1}^{\mathrm{aug}}
=\bigl((H_{n,s}(\rho),Z_{n1s})\bigr)_{s\in S}.
\]
On \(\prod_{s\in S}\Omega_{n,1s}(\rho)\), its known design mass is
\[
q_{n,1}^{\mathrm{aug}}(a)
=\prod_{s\in S}
\left\{
\binom{N_{n1s}}{\lfloor\rho N_{n1s}\rfloor}
\binom{N_{n1s}}{m_{n1s}}
\right\}^{-1}.
\]
The first-stage selector is the measurable map sending the augmented action and observed responses to the complete label \(C_n(\rho)\).  Given the first-stage history and label \(C\), define the second action by \(\mathcal A_{n,2}= (Z_{n2s})_{s:C_s=1}\), with conditional mass \(\prod_{s:C_s=1}\binom{N_{n2s}}{m_{n2s}}^{-1}\); it has no coordinate for \(C_s=0\).  This is the augmented-action representation of the two-stage design.
